					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/second-chances</guid>
				<title>A Second Chance</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/second-chances</link>
				<description>

&lt;div style=&quot;text-align: right;&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“Doctor Vasily Borodin is hereby found guilty of crimes against humanity, and is therefore sentenced to hang until death.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The courtroom erupts into applause after the verdict is stated.&lt;br /&gt;
Vasily is quiet. He feels the weight of death over life looming over him, but most of all intense hatred for these fools who’s minds were clouded by ignorance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;He knew that what he did was for the good of the human race. That all those people who suffered the torture he put them through were a means to an end to a great goal that these mindless fools around him could not begin to understand.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The FSB grab Vasily by the shoulders and take him away.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;He is thrown into a horrid excuse for a cell and he sits down on the wet floor.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The pipes above drip continuously and in a steady pace. Along with the deafening shrieks of the damned men around him that make it impossible to think clearly.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;He sits still for what seems like days, weeks possibly. Until the cell door opens. A slim figure gestures him to sit at a table in the Center of the cell. He obliges cautiously.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The man is slim and his face is emotionless. He takes out a notebook and pen.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“Hello, Doctor Borodin. I am Agent Greene. I just need to ask you a few questions about the experiment that you conducted between the date of July 3rd, 2003 to December 26th, 2016.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“Why would you ask me this? We went over the events already during the trial, yes?“&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Agent Greene taps lightly on his notebook with his pen.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“Doctor Borodin, we both know that you lied about many details during that trial. I want the truth this time. Every detail.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Vasily hesitates and looks over the man.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“Why do you need to know this?”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“I can’t tell you that yet.” Agent Greene snaps.&lt;br /&gt;
“All I can say is that if you tell me what I want to know, you will not regret it.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“I suppose I have no other choice. I will start with my theory, which I know for a fact that you will not begin to comprehend it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Agent Greene nods and readies his pen.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“Go ahead, Doctor Borodin.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“Thank you. It was January of 2003. I had been working as a psychologist at the time. I was researching the effects of physical and mental Trauma on a human subject. I had noticed a very interesting pattern. When one experiences trauma; May that be physical or mental, they grow stronger after the fact. That is when I created my magnum opus! I wondered to myself “would a human subject exposed to specific levels of physical and mental trauma become practically invincible over time?” I had to know.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Agent Greene stares blankly at Vasily.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“Doctor Borodin, such an idea is frankly ridiculous. What the hell were you thinking when you decided to throw those people in those camps?”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“Silence! Let me finish!” Vasily shouts as he slams his fist on the table.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Greene gestures Vasily to continue.&lt;br /&gt;
“I apologise. Go on.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“I then informed my trusted colleagues about my theory. Only they had the intelligence to truly understand. For months we made preparations for our experiment. We would gather subjects and lock them inside our testing area. For the next month we would put the subjects through multiple life threatening&amp;#8230;or life ending situations. We would study their growth and their ability to survive the situations that they were presented. If all of the test subjects died before the end of the decade, it would be a failure. If they lived, we weren’t able to see because our testing area was found.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Agent Greene swallowed and wrote something down in his notebook. “Could you name some of the tests that you performed on your subjects?”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“We did Quite simple things. Test one was to see if they could fend off a pack of wolves. Do not worry, most of them survived. Then we released canisters of White Phosphorus into the testing area. After this experiment I truly realised that my theory was correct! We continued with other trials for the next few years. We set all of the trees in the chamber on fire, we slowly filled their structures with water, and we told them over a speaker to kill one another for a chance to live. That last one did not go as intended, but it was a learning experience.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Agent Greene stands up. “I believe that’s all I needed to hear. Come with me, Doctor Borodin. We have a very unique offer for you.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Vasily stood up and exited the cell behind Greene. The cell door slammed shut, and the memory of the man who was condemned there was forgotten forever.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;by &lt;span class=&quot;printuser avatarhover&quot;&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mr-gibbs&quot;  &gt;&lt;img class=&quot;small&quot; src=&quot;http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7179371&amp;amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;amp;timestamp=1609559506&quot; alt=&quot;Mr Gibbs&quot; style=&quot;background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7179371)&quot; /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mr-gibbs&quot;  &gt;Mr Gibbs&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</description>
				<pubDate>Thu, 31 Dec 2020 22:59:45 +0000</pubDate>
												<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <div style="text-align: right;"></div> <p>“Doctor Vasily Borodin is hereby found guilty of crimes against humanity, and is therefore sentenced to hang until death.”</p> <p>The courtroom erupts into applause after the verdict is stated.<br /> Vasily is quiet. He feels the weight of death over life looming over him, but most of all intense hatred for these fools who’s minds were clouded by ignorance.</p> <p>He knew that what he did was for the good of the human race. That all those people who suffered the torture he put them through were a means to an end to a great goal that these mindless fools around him could not begin to understand.</p> <p>The FSB grab Vasily by the shoulders and take him away.</p> <p>He is thrown into a horrid excuse for a cell and he sits down on the wet floor.</p> <p>The pipes above drip continuously and in a steady pace. Along with the deafening shrieks of the damned men around him that make it impossible to think clearly.</p> <p>He sits still for what seems like days, weeks possibly. Until the cell door opens. A slim figure gestures him to sit at a table in the Center of the cell. He obliges cautiously.</p> <p>The man is slim and his face is emotionless. He takes out a notebook and pen.</p> <p>“Hello, Doctor Borodin. I am Agent Greene. I just need to ask you a few questions about the experiment that you conducted between the date of July 3rd, 2003 to December 26th, 2016.”</p> <p>“Why would you ask me this? We went over the events already during the trial, yes?“</p> <p>Agent Greene taps lightly on his notebook with his pen.</p> <p>“Doctor Borodin, we both know that you lied about many details during that trial. I want the truth this time. Every detail.”</p> <p>Vasily hesitates and looks over the man.</p> <p>“Why do you need to know this?”</p> <p>“I can’t tell you that yet.” Agent Greene snaps.<br /> “All I can say is that if you tell me what I want to know, you will not regret it.”</p> <p>“I suppose I have no other choice. I will start with my theory, which I know for a fact that you will not begin to comprehend it.</p> <p>Agent Greene nods and readies his pen.</p> <p>“Go ahead, Doctor Borodin.”</p> <p>“Thank you. It was January of 2003. I had been working as a psychologist at the time. I was researching the effects of physical and mental Trauma on a human subject. I had noticed a very interesting pattern. When one experiences trauma; May that be physical or mental, they grow stronger after the fact. That is when I created my magnum opus! I wondered to myself “would a human subject exposed to specific levels of physical and mental trauma become practically invincible over time?” I had to know.”</p> <p>Agent Greene stares blankly at Vasily.</p> <p>“Doctor Borodin, such an idea is frankly ridiculous. What the hell were you thinking when you decided to throw those people in those camps?”</p> <p>“Silence! Let me finish!” Vasily shouts as he slams his fist on the table.</p> <p>Greene gestures Vasily to continue.<br /> “I apologise. Go on.”</p> <p>“I then informed my trusted colleagues about my theory. Only they had the intelligence to truly understand. For months we made preparations for our experiment. We would gather subjects and lock them inside our testing area. For the next month we would put the subjects through multiple life threatening&#8230;or life ending situations. We would study their growth and their ability to survive the situations that they were presented. If all of the test subjects died before the end of the decade, it would be a failure. If they lived, we weren’t able to see because our testing area was found.”</p> <p>Agent Greene swallowed and wrote something down in his notebook. “Could you name some of the tests that you performed on your subjects?”</p> <p>“We did Quite simple things. Test one was to see if they could fend off a pack of wolves. Do not worry, most of them survived. Then we released canisters of White Phosphorus into the testing area. After this experiment I truly realised that my theory was correct! We continued with other trials for the next few years. We set all of the trees in the chamber on fire, we slowly filled their structures with water, and we told them over a speaker to kill one another for a chance to live. That last one did not go as intended, but it was a learning experience.”</p> <p>Agent Greene stands up. “I believe that’s all I needed to hear. Come with me, Doctor Borodin. We have a very unique offer for you.”</p> <p>Vasily stood up and exited the cell behind Greene. The cell door slammed shut, and the memory of the man who was condemned there was forgotten forever.</p> <p>by <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mr-gibbs" ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7179371&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609559506" alt="Mr Gibbs" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7179371)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mr-gibbs" >Mr Gibbs</a></span></p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
